urceName": "Marcus Fabius Dorsuo", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "ancient-rome", "targetName": "Ancient Rome", "context": "Marcus Fabius Dorsuo held office and lived his life within the political and geographical confines of Ancient Rome in the 4th century BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "military-tribune-with-consular-power", "sourceName": "Military Tribune with Consular Power", "verb": "DEFINES", "targetSlug": "marcus-fabius-dorsuo", "targetName": "Marcus Fabius Dorsuo", "context": "Marcus Fabius Dorsuo's primary recorded political role was as a military tribune with consular power in 379 BCE."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Roman Republic", "role": "Political center"}, {"name": "Latium, Italia", "role": "Region of activity"}, {"name": "Cremera, Roman Republic", "role": "Ancestral family tragedy site"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Marcus Fabius Dorsuo", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "marcus-fabius-dorsuo"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1225697", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcus_Fabius_Dorsuo"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Marcus Fabius Dorsuo represents the patrician elite during a critical transitional phase of the Roman Republic, contributing to the stability that allowed Rome to expand while navigating internal social conflicts. His role as a military tribune with consular power reflects a significant, albeit temporary, constitutional innovation that paved the way for plebeian integration into the highest offices.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-19T07:05:06Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32223284579", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "The gens Fabia was one of the most ancient patrician families of ancient Rome. The gens played a prominent part in history soon after the establishment of the Republic, and three brothers were invested with seven successive consulships, from 485 to 479 BC, thereby cementing the high repute of the family. Overall, the Fabii received 45 consulships during the Republic. The house derived its greatest lustre from the patriotic courage and tragic fate of the 306 Fabii in the Battle of the Cremera, 477 BC.", "newValue": "Marcus Fabius Dorsuo, a patrician of the ancient and distinguished gens Fabia, served as a military tribune with consular power in 379 BCE during a tumultuous period of the early Roman Republic. His career unfolded amidst the protracted Struggle of the Orders between patricians and plebeians, and Rome's continuous military engagements with neighboring Italic tribes. While not individually a transformative figure, his tenure as a high-ranking magistrate underscores the political compromises and military demands shaping Rome's foundational institutions in the 4th century BCE.

Elected to the collegiate magistracy, Marcus Fabius Dorsuo was one of eight military tribunes with consular power in 379 BCE, a role temporarily replacing the traditional consulship to accommodate both patrician dominance and plebeian aspirations for higher office. During his term, Rome likely continued its defensive and expansionist campaigns against peoples such as the Volsci and Aequi, while internally, the debates leading to the Licinian-Sextian Rogations — which would eventually open the consulship to plebeians — were intensifying. His specific military or political actions during his single year in office are not individually recorded, a common occurrence for many magistrates of this era.

Although Marcus Fabius Dorsuo's personal impact remains largely unchronicled, his participation in the Roman government reflects the adaptability of the patrician elite and the ongoing evolution of Republican governance. The system of military tribunes with consular power, in which he served, represented a crucial transitional phase, ultimately paving the way for the re-establishment of the two-consul system by 367 BCE, now with provisions for plebeian access. His familial connection to the Fabii, renowned for their ancestral sacrifice at the Battle of the Cremera, reinforced the gens's enduring influence even as Rome's political landscape shifted dramatically."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-19T07:05:06Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32223284579", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "350 BCE, Ancient Rome"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "350 BCE, Ancient Rome"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Struggle of the Orders created political necessity for alternative magistracies like the military tribunate with consular power.", "Rome's continuous military expansion and frequent conflicts with neighboring Italic tribes demanded multiple high-ranking commanders.", "The established prestige and historical influence of the gens Fabia ensured its members frequently attained high office in the Roman Republic."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-19T07:05:06Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32223284579", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "400 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "400 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Marcus Fabius Dorsuo's participation as a military tribune contri